ly pinged node1 and node2 over the private network.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-aedf913870b143a3\",\"cmd-87c25b637ff341bd\",\"cmd-637575d58d9c4e5d\",\"cmd-857a49a093864eec\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.92,\"summary\":\"All state-changing executor commands trace to a clear purpose: node1 DHCP server configuration (one initial failed attempt with invalid VyOS syntax that never committed and left no residue, followed by a successful commit/save), a bounded node2 connection down/up cycle used purely to verify dynamic lease reacquisition (fully self-restoring), and the three sequential node reboots performed as part of the mandated node-restart evidence protocol. No unauthorized or unexplained mutations, no failed/conflicting services, no exposed ports beyond the intended DHCP listeners on node1, and package-health checks remained clean (dpkg/dnf) before and after. Minor residue (systemd-private tmp dirs, /tmp/reboot.log) is normal reboot byproduct, not executor-caused clutter.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-472836fd0ba54d6c\",\"cmd-24d5c3a4d18a4cd0\",\"cmd-fef3e382d89348b5\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The captured evidence directly demonstrates all material outcomes of the task: node1 was configured as an authoritative Kea DHCP server on the private network (eth1, 10.50.50.0/24) shared with node2 and node3, and node2/node3's second NICs (already ipv4.method:auto with no static address) obtained dynamic leases from node1 (10.50.50.101 and 10.50.50.100 respectively). Bidirectional connectivity and lease reacquisition were verified. As an independent supplemental protocol, the executor also ran a full pre-restart/restart/post-restart evidence cycle on all three nodes, each showing a healthy baseline, a restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, and recovered DHCP/service/lease state, consistent with the fixed after-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"No before_prepare/after_prepare distinction is available for this task (prepare disabled and no baseline captured), so all observed state changes are attributed entirely to the executor by default rather than cross-verified against a distinct preparer baseline.\",\"The node1 DHCP service name in the executor's own scripted checks (kea-dhcp4-server.service) did not match the actual unit (isc-kea-dhcp4-server.service), producing a spurious inactive/not-found result in one intermediate command; this was a diagnostic naming mismatch, not evidence of an actual service problem, since the correctly-named unit was confirmed active in subsequent evidence.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-node1-dhcp-server","requirement":"node1 (VyOS) is configured as an authoritative DHCP server on the private network shared with node2 and node3","status":"satisfied","summary":"After an initial failed commit attempt due to invalid config syntax, node1 was successfully configured with eth1 address 10.50.50.254/24, shared-network PRIVATE, subnet 10.50.50.0/24 with pool 10.50.50.100-200 and default-router/name-server options; commit and save succeeded. show configuration commands confirms the committed config, and the isc-kea-dhcp4-server.service was independently confirmed active both immediately after configuration and again after a full reboot of node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24d5c3a4d18a4cd0","cmd-637575d58d9c4e5d","cmd-94ed8fb080b64dca","cmd-24ee1a1e4af04210"]},{"id":"req-2-node2-dynamic","requirement":"node2 obtains its address on the shared private network from node1 via DHCP rather than a static assignment","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2's Wired connection 2 (enp8s0) was confirmed ipv4.method:auto with no static ipv4.addresses configured be